.TH LD09 1 2026-11-09 "bcc09 0.8.0" "bcc09 Manual"
.SH NAME
ld09 \- linker for the Motorola 6809
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ld09
.RB [ \-Mdimstvy [ \- ]]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RB [ \-T\c
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-D\c
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-H\c
.IR size ]
.RB [ \-L\c
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-C\c
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-l\c
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-O\c
.IR file ]
.IR file ...
.br
.B ld09 \-r \-o
.I archive
.IR object ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ld09
links object files from
.BR as09 (1),
and members of libraries (archives made by
.BR ar09 (1)),
into an executable: an a.out file, which
.BR objcopy09 (1)
turns into what a machine loads.
It is dev86's ld86 for the 6809; the compiler driver
.BR bcc09 (1)
runs it.
.PP
.B ld09
loads only the modules that something refers to, from the entry
symbols (see
.B ENTRY
in
.BR as09 (1))
on: an object file named on the command line, like a library's
member, is left out if nothing needs it.
A library is searched only for what is undefined when it is read, so
libraries come after the files that use them.
.PP
Of the 16 segments
.RB ( LOC
0 to 15 in
.BR as09 (1)),
0 and 1 are code, placed at the text address; 2 (the direct page,
which must fit in one 256-byte page) and 3 (the data, then the BSS)
are placed after the code, or at the data address; 4 to 15 are stored
after the code, each where its
.BI __seg n DL
symbol says.
.B ld09
defines symbols for the program to find its parts:
.B __btext
and
.B __etext
(the code's start and end),
.B __bdata
and
.B __edata
(the data's),
.B __end
(the BSS's end),
.B __heap_top
(the end plus
.BR \-H 's
size), and
.BI __seg n DL\c
,
.BI __seg n DH
(segment
.IR n 's
start and end).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
The executable (a.out by default).
.TP
.BI \-T address
The address the code is linked at (0 by default).
.TP
.BI \-D address
The address the data is linked at, rather than straight after the
code; it makes the data separate
.RB ( \-i ).
.TP
.BI \-H size
Room after the BSS, for the heap (or OS-9's stack: see
.BR objcopy09 (1)).
.IP
The addresses and the size are hexadecimal, with or without a
.B 0x
(so
.B \-T3000
and
.B \-T0x3000
are the same).
.TP
.B \-i
Separate code and data: the data is linked at 0 (or at
.BR \-D 's
address) instead of after the code, as OS-9's programs reach their data
through a register.
.TP
.BI \-l name
Link the library
.RI lib name .a,
from the library directories.
.TP
.BI \-L dir
Search
.I dir
for libraries (and start-up files) too.
.TP
.BI \-C name
Link the start-up file
.RI crt name .o,
from the library directories, first.
.TP
.BI \-O file
Link the library
.IR file ,
from the library directories.
.TP
.B \-s
Leave the symbols out of the executable.
.TP
.B \-y
Write each symbol's whole name, in the 8-character entries of an a.out
symbol table and the entries after them (the default;
.B \-y\-
cuts names to 8 characters).
.TP
.B \-d
Write the code and data alone, with no a.out header (and no symbols).
.TP
.B \-m
Print the modules linked.
.TP
.B \-M
Print the symbols linked.
.TP
.B \-t
Trace the files and modules as they are read.
.TP
.B \-r
Make an archive of the objects, for
.BR ar09 (1)
or a later link, rather than linking them (with
.B \-o
required).
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version (as in
.BR "ld09 0.8.0" )
on standard output, and exit with status 0.
.PP
A flag option is turned off by following it with
.BR \- ,
as in
.BR \-y\- .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if the link succeeded, 1 otherwise (an undefined symbol, a file that
can't be read).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bcc09 (1),
.BR as09 (1),
.BR ar09 (1),
.BR objdump09 (1),
.BR objcopy09 (1)
